msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:140
msgid "Open the ``01-Flame_alert_system.ino`` file under the path of ``ultimate-sensor-kit\\code\\iot_project_espc201\\wifi\\01-Flame_alert_system``, or copy this code into **Arduino IDE**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:143
msgid "The three Blynk lines were copied in :ref:`c201_iot_blynk_start`. Every project of this kit uses the same device, so they never have to be changed - only the Wi-Fi settings below are project specific."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:153
msgid "You also need to enter the ``ssid`` and ``password`` of the WiFi you are using."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:160
msgid "After selecting the correct board and port, click the **Upload** button. Open the Serial monitor(set baudrate to 115200) and wait for a prompt such as a successful connection to appear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:165
msgid "Now, Blynk will show the data read from flame sensor. In the label widget, you can see the value read by the flame sensor. When the displayed value is 1, the background of the label will be shown in green. When the value is 0, the background of the label will be shown in red and Blynk will send you an alert email."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:170
msgid "If you want to use Blynk on mobile devices, please refer to :ref:`blynk_mobile`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:174
msgid "4. Code explanation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:176
msgid "**Blynk account and libraries**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:178
msgid "The first three lines tell the board which Blynk device it belongs to. They were copied in the Get Started chapter and are the same in every project of this kit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:192
msgid "**The serial port that talks to the module**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:194
msgid "The ESPC2-01 does not run your sketch - it works as a modem that receives AT commands. On the R4 it is wired to D0/D1, which is ``Serial1``, a hardware serial port, so there are no bit errors at 115200 baud."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:204
msgid "**Wi-Fi credentials, sensor pin and timer**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:214
msgid "The flame module outputs a plain digital level, so pin 8 is read with ``digitalRead()``. ``BlynkTimer`` is a software timer from the Blynk library: it runs a function on a schedule without ``delay()``, which would freeze the connection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:218
msgid "**espModuleInit() - wake the module up first**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:220
msgid "The module needs about a second to boot and prints its start-up log on the very same wire. If the sketch starts sending AT commands too early, the replies get mixed with that log and Blynk cannot read them. This function waits for the word ``ready``, then sends ``ATE0`` to switch the echo off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:242
msgid "``ATE0`` is not optional: with echo on, every reply starts with the command you just sent, so the Blynk parser sees a line of garbage before the real answer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:245
msgid "**setup()**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:263
msgid "``timer.setInterval(1000L, myTimerEvent)`` calls the function every 1000 ms. Change that number to report faster or slower."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:265
msgid "``Blynk.config()`` stores the token, ``Blynk.connectWiFi()`` then brings the module online. You could use one line, ``Blynk.begin(...)``, but doing it in two steps lets the module finish booting first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:269
msgid "**loop()**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:278
msgid "``Blynk.run()`` keeps the connection alive and ``timer.run()`` fires the scheduled function. Nothing in the loop may block, or the platform drops the device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:281
msgid "**sendData() - read the pin and report it**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:295
msgid "``Blynk.virtualWrite(V0, data)`` pushes the value to datastream **V0**, which is what the label on the dashboard shows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:297
msgid "The flame module is **active low**: it reads ``0`` when a flame is close and ``1`` when the air is clear. That is why the sketch checks ``data == 0``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_espc201/wifi_espc201/01-iot_Flame_alert_system.rst:299
msgid "``Blynk.logEvent(\"flame_detected\")`` sends the event that pops the notification up. The string must match the **EVENT CODE** you created earlier, character for character."
msgstr ""
